+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — humantime
+
+Property-based tests were added with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 following the hegel skill
+workflow. Tests live in the existing test modules of `src/duration.rs` and
+`src/date.rs` (no separate test files, per the skill). The four pre-existing
+`rand`-loop tests (`random_second`, `random_any`, `random_past`,
+`random_wide_range`) were evolved into hegel properties with broader input
+ranges, per the skill's guidance on evolving rand-based tests.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### `src/duration.rs`
+
+1. **Format→parse roundtrip over the full `Duration` domain** —
+   `prop_format_duration_parse_roundtrip`. Evidence: `format_duration` docs:
+   "this format is guaranteed to have same value when using parse_duration".
+   Evolved from `random_second`/`random_any`, widened from
+   `0..253_370_764_800` seconds to the full `u64` range (with `u64::MAX`
+   boosted via `one_of!` + `sampled_from`), plus arbitrary subsecond nanos.
+
+2. **Parse robustness (no panic) on arbitrary Unicode text** —
+   `prop_parse_duration_never_panics`. Evidence: `parse_duration` returns
+   `Result` with a documented `Error` enum; the parser slices `src[start..end]`
+   with byte offsets derived from char iteration, so Unicode input is the risky
+   case.
+
+3. **Error positions are valid pointers into the input** —
+   `prop_parse_duration_error_positions_are_valid`. Evidence: `Error` docs say
+   the fields are byte offsets / start-end of a slice of the original string,
+   and `UnknownUnit.unit` is "the unit verbatim". Asserts offsets are
+   `<= s.len()`, lie on char boundaries, and `&s[start..end] == unit`.
+   (Offset `== s.len()` is deliberately allowed: inputs like `"1. "` report
+   end-of-input as the error position.)
+
+4. **Single `<integer><unit>` spans equal the documented multiplier or
+   overflow cleanly** — `prop_single_integer_unit_matches_documented_multiplier`.
+   Evidence: the unit/multiplier table in the `parse_duration` docs (months =
+   30.44 days, years = 365.25 days), the `test_units` and `test_overlow` unit
+   tests, and the documented `NumberOverflow` error. Samples all 41 accepted
+   unit spellings ("generate configurations, not just payloads") with the full
+   `u64` value range; expects `Ok(n * mult)` when representable, else
+   `Err(NumberOverflow)`.
+
+5. **9-digit fractional seconds denote exactly those nanoseconds** —
+   `prop_fractional_seconds_parse_to_exact_nanos`. Evidence: the
+   `test_fractional_units` examples (e.g. `"1.000000002s"` == 2 ns).
+   `parse_duration("{s}.{n:09}s") == Duration::new(s, n)` for any `u64` s and
+   any nanos.
+
+### `src/date.rs`
+
+6. **Whole-second format/parse agrees with the `time` crate oracle** —
+   `prop_rfc3339_agrees_with_time_crate`. Evidence: the crate's own
+   `random_past`/`random_wide_range` tests used exactly this oracle; evolved to
+   hegel, covering `0..=max::SECONDS` (last second of year 9999) with
+   boundaries boosted.
+
+7. **`parse_rfc3339(format_rfc3339(t)) == t` including subsecond precision** —
+   `prop_rfc3339_format_parse_roundtrip`. Evidence: the paired
+   format/parse API, the `Timestamp` wrapper's `Display`/`FromStr` pair, and
+   existing roundtrip unit tests (`all_86400_seconds`, `first_731_days`).
+
+8. **`format_rfc3339_nanos` output is RFC 3339 the `time` crate parses to the
+   same instant (nanosecond-exact)** —
+   `prop_rfc3339_nanos_output_parses_with_time_crate`. Evidence: function docs
+   ("Format an RFC3339 timestamp"); this is the RFC 3339 conformance check for
+   the fractional-seconds path, which the whole-second oracle test misses.
+
+9. **Fixed-precision formatters truncate and parse back to the truncated
+   value** — `prop_fixed_precision_formatters_truncate`. Evidence: the
+   formatter docs ("always shows milliseconds/microseconds/…") and the micros
+   smoke test showing truncation (456_123_000 ns → `.456123`). Draws the
+   precision (seconds/millis/micros/nanos) as part of the input.
+
+10. **Strict parser never panics on arbitrary input** —
+    `prop_parse_rfc3339_never_panics`. Evidence: `Result` return type; the
+    parser does raw byte indexing after length checks ("for careless slicing"),
+    so mutated near-valid strings are the risky case. Generator mixes the
+    crate's own formatter output, regex-shaped near-timestamps, arbitrary
+    Unicode text, and single-byte corruptions (generalizing `break_data`).
+
+11. **Weak parser never panics on arbitrary input** —
+    `prop_parse_rfc3339_weak_never_panics`. Same evidence/generator as 10.
+
+12. **Weak parser accepts everything the strict parser accepts, with the same
+    value** — `prop_weak_accepts_everything_strict_accepts`. Evidence:
+    `parse_rfc3339_weak` docs, point 3: "Anything valid for parse_rfc3339 is
+    valid for this function".
+
+13. **Strict parser rejects a valid timestamp followed by trailing garbage** —
+    `prop_parse_rfc3339_rejects_trailing_garbage`. Evidence: `parse_rfc3339`
+    docs ("Parse RFC3339 timestamp", "for strings generated programmatically");
+    a timestamp plus trailing bytes is not an RFC 3339 timestamp, and the
+    `break_data` unit test shows corrupted inputs are expected to fail.
+    **FAILS — suspected bug, see below.**
+
+14. **Weak parser rejects non-zero UTC offsets** —
+    `prop_parse_rfc3339_weak_rejects_nonzero_offsets`. Evidence: docs of both
+    parsers: "The UTC timezone can be indicated with Z or +00:00",
+    "Unsupported feature: localized timestamps. Only UTC is supported."
+    Silently reading `+05:00` as UTC would return a wrong instant; the crate's
+    own `weak_parse_offset_00` test *intends* to assert `+02:00` fails (though
+    it mistakenly calls the strict parser — see Suspected bugs).
+    **FAILS — suspected bug, see below.**
+
+## Test results
+
+Run with `cargo test` (default 100 cases per property). An exploratory run at
+`test_cases = 1000` (10x) was also done per the skill; it found no additional
+failures. Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting the roundtrip
+assertion and confirming hegel reported a shrunk counterexample.
+
