+
+All 34 pre-existing unit tests and 5 doc tests still pass.
+Overall: `46 passed; 2 failed`.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Bug 1: `parse_rfc3339` accepts trailing garbage after the `Z`
+
+- **Minimal counterexample (hegel-shrunk):**
+  `parse_rfc3339("1970-01-01T00:00:00ZZ")` returns `Ok(UNIX_EPOCH)` instead of
+  an error. Anything matching "valid 19-char timestamp + `Z` + up to 5 junk
+  bytes ending in `Z`" is accepted, e.g. `"1970-01-01T00:00:00Zx24Z"`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** `parse_rfc3339` is documented as the strict,
+  RFC 3339, machine-oriented parser (in contrast to `parse_rfc3339_weak` "for
+  human input"). `"1970-01-01T00:00:00ZZ"` is not an RFC 3339 timestamp.
+- **Code path at fault:** `src/date.rs`. The strict wrapper (line ~89) only
+  checks `b[10] == b'T'` and that the string *ends* with `Z` or `+00:00`, then
+  delegates to `parse_rfc3339_weak`. In the weak parser's no-fraction branch
+  (line ~187), a string is accepted whenever
+  `b.len() <= 25 && b[19] == b'Z'` — the bytes between position 20 and the end
+  are never inspected. So length-21..=25 strings with `Z` at position 19 and a
+  final `Z` pass both layers. (The weak parser consequently also accepts
+  trailing garbage such as `"1970-01-01 00:00:00Z123"`, but the docs give it
+  more latitude.)
+
+### Bug 2: `parse_rfc3339_weak` silently treats non-zero (or garbage) UTC offsets as UTC when fractional seconds are present
+
+- **Minimal counterexample (hegel-shrunk):**
+  `parse_rfc3339_weak("1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000000+00:01")` returns
+  `Ok(UNIX_EPOCH)` — the `+00:01` offset is silently ignored, yielding an
+  instant that is wrong by one minute. Any offset works:
+  `"...00.5+23:59"`, and even non-numeric junk like `"...00.5+ab:cd"`, are all
+  read as UTC. Without a fractional part the same inputs are correctly
+  rejected (`b[19..]` is compared against `b"+00:00"` exactly).
+- **Why it's a library bug:** the docs say "Only UTC is supported, even if `Z`
+  is not specified" and list `+00:00` as the only offset spelling; returning a
+  silently-wrong instant for `+05:00` cannot be intended "support". The
+  crate's own `weak_parse_offset_00` test contains
+  `parse_rfc3339("1970-01-01 00:00:00.0000123+02:00").unwrap_err()` lines in
+  its "invalid" block whose evident intent is to assert this — but they call
+  the *strict* parser (which rejects the string for the unrelated reason that
+  `b[10]` is a space), so the bug goes unnoticed. That looks like a bug in the
+  existing test as well.
+- **Code path at fault:** `src/date.rs`, fractional-seconds loop (lines
+  ~176–182): on seeing `b'+'` at index `b.len() - 6` it just `break`s — it
+  validates the *position* of the `+` but never that the following five bytes
+  are `00:00`.
+- **Interaction:** `parse_rfc3339` (strict) is shielded from Bug 2 by its
+  `ends_with("+00:00")` pre-check, so this is weak-parser-only.
+
+Per the task's ground rules, no library code was changed; both tests are left
+in place, failing.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"`InvalidCharacter`/`NumberExpected` offsets point strictly *at* a
+  character (`off < s.len()`)"** — the `Error` docs say the field is "byte
+  offset of the (erroneous) character", but code reading showed inputs like
+  `"1. "` report `off == s.len()` (end of input, where a digit was expected
+  but the string ended). Rather than assert the letter of the doc and file a
+  dubious bug, the committed property asserts the defensible contract: offsets
+  are `<= len` and on char boundaries, i.e. always safe for callers to slice
+  and display. The relaxation is documented in the test comment.
+- **"`NumberExpected(off)` points at the erroneous character"** (exact
+  position) — abandoned after reading `parse_first_char`: the offset is
+  captured *before* skipping leading whitespace, so for `" x"` the reported
+  offset is 0, not 1. Position-exactness is not actually promised strongly
+  enough to test; only the boundary-validity property above was kept.
+- Nothing else was tried and dropped; in particular the two failing properties
+  were kept (not weakened) because the evidence for them is direct
+  documentation text.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well end-to-end: MSRV gotcha, crate-vs-package
+naming, composite generators, `one_of!` boundary boosting, the liveness check,
+and the 10x exploratory run were all documented and all needed. Specific
+notes:
+
+1. **The MSRV note in `references/rust/reference.md` Setup was exactly right.**
+   `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed against humantime's `rust-version = 1.60`
+   with the predicted error, and `--ignore-rust-version` fixed it. Having the
+   exact error text in the reference made this a 30-second, zero-doubt step.
+
+2. **Counterexample output labels draws poorly for booleans.** The failure
+   for `prop_parse_rfc3339_weak_rejects_nonzero_offsets` printed
+   `let draw_1 = true;` for an unnamed boolean draw while the other draws got
+   their `let` binding names (`let secs = 0;`). The reference could mention
+   how draw names are derived (apparently from the binding) and that
+   `tc.draw` results not bound directly to a variable get positional names —
+   worth knowing when reading counterexamples. Not a blocker.
+
+3. **"Evolve existing rand tests" guidance was actionable but could say what
+   to do with the now-unused `rand` dev-dependency.** After evolving the four
+   rand-loop tests, `rand` remains in `[dev-dependencies]` unused (silently —
+   cargo doesn't warn). I left it in place to keep the diff minimal; a
+   sentence in `evolving-tests.md`/porting docs on whether to drop the
+   dependency would remove the judgment call. (I did not load
+   `evolving-tests.md` — SKILL.md positions it for unit-test evolution and the
+   rand-loop bullet right above it was self-sufficient; if that file also
+   covers the dependency question, a pointer from the rand bullet would help.)
+
+4. **Oracle dev-dependencies may need extra features.** Using the `time` crate
+   as an oracle required adding its `parsing` feature (`UtcDateTime::parse`
+   didn't exist with only `formatting`). This is ordinary Rust friction, but
+   the skill's oracle/reference-implementation advice could note that enabling
+   extra *features on existing dev-dependencies* is a normal part of setting up
+   an oracle.
+
+5. **`extras.md`'s load condition is slightly ambiguous for this case.** The
+   trigger is "the code under test uses chrono/jiff/serde_json/rand" — here
+   the *library* uses none of them but the *tests* used `rand` and the benches
+   use `chrono`. I loaded it, concluded none of the integrations were needed
+   (hegel generators replace the RNG entirely when evolving rand *tests*, as
+   opposed to code under test that *takes* an RNG), and moved on. One
+   clarifying sentence — "tests that merely use `rand` to generate inputs
+   don't need the `rand` feature; replace the RNG with hegel draws" — would
+   have saved the detour. (The information is technically derivable from
+   SKILL.md's "Handling Randomness" section, but only by inference.)
+
+6. **The skill's ordering paid off.** "Read the source / existing tests for
+   evidence" (step 2) is what surfaced both real bugs: the suspicious
+   `b.len() > 25 || b[19] != b'Z'` length check and the `+`-at-`len-6` break
+   were visible in the source, and the property catalogue's "parse robustness /
+   roundtrip / conformance" angles turned them into failing tests on the first
+   run. The `test_cases = 1000` exploratory run wasn't needed for these (both
+   fail at the default count thanks to the boosted/targeted generators), but
+   it was cheap.
+
+7. **Minor: no global case-count override (gotcha 13) makes the 10x run
+   clunky.** `sed`-ing 14 attributes and reverting works but is inelegant;
+   if hegel ever grows an env-var override, the skill should mention it.
